WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.340
Dependency security is part of application security.

2
00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:15.800
NuGet packages, transitive dependencies, analyzers, build tools, container images, runtime versions, and platform libraries all become part of the application's trusted base.

3
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:22.140
Adding or upgrading a package should be reviewed like any other code change that can affect behavior.

4
00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:29.060
Version pinning or lock-file practices can help teams make builds repeatable and review dependency changes intentionally.

5
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:31.880
The goal is not to freeze software forever.

6
00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:39.900
The goal is to know what changed, why it changed, who reviewed it, and how the team will patch when security updates are needed.

7
00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.900
CI/CD systems handle sensitive operations.

8
00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:53.400
Build pipelines may access source, secrets, signing material, deployment credentials, cloud identities, package feeds, and production environments.

9
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.500
Those permissions should be scoped, audited, and protected.

10
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:01.600
A pipeline that can deploy the application is a privileged part of the system.

11
00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:07.720
Environment-specific configuration should remain outside source code and should be managed through deployment systems.

12
00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:16.140
Development settings, test credentials, staging endpoints, production keys, and diagnostic flags should not blur together.

13
00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:21.360
The application should make it hard to accidentally ship development assumptions into production.

14
00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:34.680
Deployment security includes containers, cloud services, runtime settings, network exposure, TLS configuration, managed identities, logging sinks, health checks, and patch cadence.

15
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:40.060
Treat deployment as part of the application, not an afterthought owned by someone else.

16
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:44.320
Security review should be part of change management and release readiness.

17
00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:47.000
Patching is ongoing engineering work.

18
00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:54.460
.NET runtimes, dependencies, operating systems, base images, and cloud services all change over time.

19
00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:03.920
A secure C sharp workflow includes monitoring for updates, testing patches, reviewing release impact, and deploying fixes through repeatable processes.
